#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542392 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=2$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=5-2\\cdot y.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c0/c015a0e708a8f931bc5d8857b69a734490518d79afb1f493183940f6d888ad1d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=5-2\\cdot y=-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+5.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le -2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (4\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{2}}\\int_{0}^{-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+5}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{2}}4\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+5}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{2}}4\\cdot (r)^{2}((-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+5)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{2}}-8\\cdot (r)^{3}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+20\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[-2\\cdot (r)^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+\\frac{20}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{2}}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-2\\cdot ((\\sqrt{2}))^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))-(-2\\cdot ((0))^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))+(\\frac{20}{3}\\cdot ((\\sqrt{2}))^{3})-(\\frac{20}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-8\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))-(0)+(\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{2})-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}-8\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\theta\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+8\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(\\frac{80}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\sqrt{2})-(0)+(8)-(8) \\\\ &=\\frac{80}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542404 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 2\\cdot x\\cdot z\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=6$ for $y\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=-x$ and $z=x+10.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/04/0472caa4e40aac437429c5f542f8c7c155d69ff1a348e1d8a4c37b4ced9b472e.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{6}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-x=-r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta),$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x+10=r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+10.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{6},\\ 0\\le\\theta<\\pi,\\ -r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)\\le z\\le r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+10\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 2\\cdot x\\cdot z\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (2\\cdot z\\cdot r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{6}}\\int_{-r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)}^{r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+10}2\\cdot z\\cdot (r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{6}}(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)[(z)^2]_{z=-r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)}^{z=r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+10}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{6}}(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)((r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+10)^2-(-r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{6}}(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)((r)^{2}\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2}+20\\cdot r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+100-((r)^{2}\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2}))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{6}}20\\cdot (r)^{3}\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2}+100\\cdot (r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}[5\\cdot (r)^{4}\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2}+\\frac{100}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)]_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{6}}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}(5\\cdot ((\\sqrt{6}))^{4}\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2})-(5\\cdot ((0))^{4}\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2})+(\\frac{100}{3}\\cdot ((\\sqrt{6}))^{3}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))-(\\frac{100}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}(180\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2})-(0)+(200\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}180\\cdot (\\cos(\\theta))^{2}+200\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}180(\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)))+200\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}200\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)+90\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+90\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[90\\cdot \\theta+200\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+45\\cdot \\sin(2\\cdot \\theta)]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=\\pi} \\\\ &=(90\\cdot \\pi)-(0)+(0)-(0)+(0)-(0) \\\\ &=90\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542409 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c7fe69ab2583c066df73bbf6719e4c58fea1ed565e345bc62c11b7ebc779508b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4=4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (4\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\int_{0}^{4}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}((4)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}16\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot ((1))^{3})-(\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\frac{16}{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542430 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=4$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/05/0505715b5e78700e63d98fc2c1db8e7d4a3074b3e06f4202de1ce5a6831f7571.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (4\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}\\int_{0}^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}4\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}4\\cdot (r)^{2}((1)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=2}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot ((2))^{3})-(\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\frac{32}{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542514 :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=3 x z^{2} - 3 y^{3}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨- 3 t,3,\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}⟩$ at the point $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x z^{2} - 3 y^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x z^{2} - 3 y^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3 x z^{2} - 3 y^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 z^{2},- 9 y^{2},6 x z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=- 3 t$, $y=3$, $z=\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨3 \\left(\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}\\right)^{2},- 9 \\cdot 3^{2},6 \\left(- 3 t\\right) \\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 t^{2} - 9,-81,- 18 t \\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3 t^{2} - 9,-81,- 18 t \\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(3 t^{2} - 9\\right)\\cdot \\left(-3\\right)+\\left(-81\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(- 18 t \\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}}\\right) \\\\  & =- 9 t^{2} + 27+0- 18 t^{2} \\\\  & =- 27 t^{2} + 27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(2))] & =- 27 (2)^{2} + 27 \\\\  & =-9+0-72 \\\\  & =-81.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542527 :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=x^{2} - 2 y^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,- 3 t + 1⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} - 2 y^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} - 2 y^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 x,- 4 y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t$, $y=- 3 t + 1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2 t,- 4 \\left(- 3 t + 1\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 t,12 t - 4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 t + 1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2 t,12 t - 4⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(2 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(12 t - 4\\right)\\cdot \\left(-3\\right) \\\\  & =- 34 t + 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =- 34 (1) + 12 \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542538 :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨- t + 2,2 t⟩$ at the point $t=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 6 x,6 y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=- t + 2$, $y=2 t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨- 6 \\left(- t + 2\\right),6 \\cdot 2 t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6 t - 12,12 t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- t + 2),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨6 t - 12,12 t⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(6 t - 12\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right)+\\left(12 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\  & =18 t + 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-1))] & =18 (-1) + 12 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542540 :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} - 3 y^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨- 3 t + 2,2 t - 1⟩$ at the point $t=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{2} - 3 y^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{2} - 3 y^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6 x,- 6 y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=- 3 t + 2$, $y=2 t - 1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨6 \\left(- 3 t + 2\\right),- 6 \\left(2 t - 1\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 18 t + 12,- 12 t + 6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 t + 2),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t - 1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨- 18 t + 12,- 12 t + 6⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(- 18 t + 12\\right)\\cdot \\left(-3\\right)+\\left(- 12 t + 6\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\  & =30 t - 24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-2))] & =30 (-2) - 24 \\\\  & =-84.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542541 :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=x^{2} - y^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨- 3 t + 1,- t - 1⟩$ at the point $t=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} - y^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} - y^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 x,- 2 y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=- 3 t + 1$, $y=- t - 1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2 \\left(- 3 t + 1\\right),- 2 \\left(- t - 1\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 6 t + 2,2 t + 2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 t + 1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- t - 1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨- 6 t + 2,2 t + 2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(- 6 t + 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(-3\\right)+\\left(2 t + 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\  & =16 t - 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-2))] & =16 (-2) - 8 \\\\  & =-40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542549 :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t - 2,3 t + 2⟩$ at the point $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 3 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 3 x^{2} - 2 y^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 6 x,- 4 y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t - 2$, $y=3 t + 2$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨- 6 \\left(t - 2\\right),- 4 \\left(3 t + 2\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 6 t + 12,- 12 t - 8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t - 2),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t + 2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨- 6 t + 12,- 12 t - 8⟩\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(- 6 t + 12\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 12 t - 8\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right) \\\\  & =- 42 t - 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =- 42 (0) - 12 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542606 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x$ along the line $y=2 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(2 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(t)(\\sqrt{5})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{\\sqrt{5} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\left((3)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\left(9-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542607 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x \\left(y - 1\\right)$ along the line $y=2 x + 1,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2 t + 1.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(2 t + 1)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x \\left(y - 1\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2}(2 t^{2})(\\sqrt{5})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5} t^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\left((2)^{3}-(0)^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\left(8-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542608 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=5 x$ along the line $y=6 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 7.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=6 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,6 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 7\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(6 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(6)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(6)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{37}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=5 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=5 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{7}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{7}(5 t)(\\sqrt{37})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{7} \\\\ &=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37}}{2}\\left((7)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37}}{2}\\left(49-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542609 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=3 x$ along the line $y=4 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 5.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=4 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,4 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 5\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(4 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(4)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(4)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{5}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{5}(3 t)(\\sqrt{17})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{5} \\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{2}\\left((5)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{2}\\left(25-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542610 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)$ along the line $y=3x+2,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,3t+2⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)j \\\\  & =i+3\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{2}+(3t+2)={t}^{2}+3t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\,(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+2t){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{8}{3}+6+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542611 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$ along the curve $y={e}^{x},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({e}^{t})^{2}=2{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{2t}$, $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{17}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({17}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(17 \\sqrt{17}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542612 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=4 x$ along the line $y=5 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 6.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=5 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,5 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(5 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(5)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(5)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=4 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{6}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{6}(4 t)(\\sqrt{26})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[2 \\sqrt{26} t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{6} \\\\ &=2 \\sqrt{26}\\left((6)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=2 \\sqrt{26}\\left(36-0\\right) \\\\ &=72 \\sqrt{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542613 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2 x$ along the line $y=3 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,3 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(3 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(3)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(3)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{4}(2 t)(\\sqrt{10})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\sqrt{10} t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{4} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\left((4)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\left(16-0\\right) \\\\ &=16 \\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543680 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/4f/4ff66220bf03e9c5dde372e20aa060fa26a4a57ab481a61845726fde8ca25046.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta-2)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta+r^2\\sin^2\\theta-4r\\sin\\theta+4&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-4\\sin\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/88/882f7487d40a724c331aebb3dd954b8beace88ff5a2e65b4b2b9abedd74885dc.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=0$ and $\\theta=\\pi.$\n\n![](images/41/410279d76b3d76135133c63fe19003839b783a5d4177aff6744bd950fcbe93bd.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le\\theta\\le \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543704 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+y^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/86/866f3162cd881e997f28cea68fe22fc90b8ef011b6d8beff00ef38d9a69d2eb9.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+y^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+y^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta-2)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta-4r\\cos\\theta+4+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-4\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/6c/6c841a1dc73b8a68ba8d51bb4d0f8d1ff051f8d1c0bafdfdbfac134ccbc651aa.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/08/083360143d05d210dcbd11ed86c3a5cc0f2510beeecb68a210e9f52632609867.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543707 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+2)^{2}+y^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/3a/3a77e854cc9553675752e3e4e231c8915d390091a4f4fa22cee2eb5ffe279c3e.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(-2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x+2)^{2}+y^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+y^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta+2)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta+4r\\cos\\theta+4+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)+4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2+4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r+4\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/a9/a934a520dfc672601e31dd73fc71a06505f41b6088325a30b49f4d7d99d0f0d7.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/d5/d5b43bd12ba3ca3b3dd69560c728f9c7b16d2d8331702cc3921e8457f53c294f.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543716 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}\\le 9\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/74/74735ff47608d314c82b66367404f6d2bb79c0c1e34b034083ebcd2d74dc55c0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $3$ centered at $(3,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}&=9\\\\(r\\cos\\theta-3)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=9\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta-6r\\cos\\theta+9+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=9\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-6r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-6r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-6\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/8b/8bafd3851f0378006c6e851b9bb60ab4bbcb1dadafffe4d5b5082d304ca7fed7.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/cc/ccdbd4ea144224d5793d4c48018147ca939cba611e5ea38ac649295218ca2f07.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543720 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle D = \\{(x, y): (x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} + y^{2} \\le \\frac{9}{4}\\},\n$$ then $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)(x^{2} - y^{2})dA =\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/d0/d01daaf1f81b8dd2b2ab8d01bbbe5a7dd1f4262eb7de9d3977b8c1e104a90ec0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(\\frac{3}{2},0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+y^{2}&=\\frac{9}{4}\\\\(r\\cos\\theta-\\frac{3}{2})^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=\\frac{9}{4}\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta-3r\\cos\\theta+\\frac{9}{4}+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=\\frac{9}{4}\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-3r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-3r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-3\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/ac/ac0374d74edf7f4490f7c160b929b9dc5d03d1324d7cb79dc691a5ca21248e35.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/c6/c674039d133057398ff5fd98770479e9fba7866524ead09c78adba2c2ac5cb80.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (- r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543721 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}\\le \\frac{9}{4}\\},\n$$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/07/074eb42aff2c78d93136fc9e56b2420450f72c5d34c6b12da8bf84e080db75ea.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(0,\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/ce/cea2b2ddd6b211073b579d71bd8ed08405fa04e354825a124acb71bedf413295.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/bb/bbe07b71b3c82cde2e9175b06904905206984b04ca9a8ccd746b051212f5e8d8.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543726 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/57/57ffd44d5406aafa448571f9a56eebded9a9916def605c08eb4eb1ad1e0df56c.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}+(y+2)^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta+2)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta+r^2\\sin^2\\theta+4r\\sin\\theta+4&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)+4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r^2+4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r(r+4\\sin\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/0b/0b3138e7b6599e8174ccd46648b334b18a505757b845e53af59d0b98e3b4c92b.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=\\pi$ and $\\theta=2 \\pi.$\n\n![](images/11/11dcaf5545227d8231eef23ee29ce52723be103c8a209a578927cacb83d56384.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543730 :question/problem "![](images/1f/1fef351611744d83aac26742bada76ee0eb7f4beef06698d8b4b02061aa8045b.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $\\frac{14}{3}$ and $\\frac{5}{4}:$ \n\n![](images/d6/d64fa6b26b1730514b90b28989773cd8996b8deda2dd80bece2e48def3859320.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{14}{3}}^{2}+{\\frac{5}{4}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{196}{9}+\\frac{25}{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3361}{144}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3361}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{3361}}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543741 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdccb48ecbf30257f5dce2b1d8f9be79cfbc945ed804f0ceef75e519c0292f23.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\frac{9}{2}:$ \n\n![](images/3a/3ab15ec00cb05ea65a36d59f88246cb56347ea6dc61ff2e589882a4ab92f1919.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{9}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{81}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{45}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543748 :question/problem "![](images/c5/c515af7e27657f8bbf832fd35ed69989eb3c7655e1b16b4157b6dc101e827f32.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $\\frac{10}{3}$ and $\\frac{17}{4}:$ \n\n![](images/7c/7cd1670a27c1d84aea03718ca99d611caa825bcc2f16b9955dd35f3106325c87.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{10}{3}}^{2}+{\\frac{17}{4}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{100}{9}+\\frac{289}{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4201}{144}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4201}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{4201}}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543752 :question/problem "![](images/61/61c9e7095d0b1dfdc2b302a5ca916d05f6a6dcad206be5eb67550246213a9743.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $\\frac{5}{4}$ and $\\frac{8}{3}:$ \n\n![](images/2d/2d17bae26ea31dc9b70309cb56781b054c3086e21f5fbbb642e85c231ae5b451.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{4}}^{2}+{\\frac{8}{3}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{16}+\\frac{64}{9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1249}{144}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1249}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{1249}}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543764 :question/problem "![](images/55/5561697837153becfab9a6203b3987d2a63399b5bccb1bcea3dbff3c8585c8e7.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $\\frac{15}{2}$ and $\\frac{11}{2}:$ \n\n![](images/c3/c3c7adcca3b7855b0545e51b72625bd5addaa06db20e4b3de88bed81d47b9d29.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{15}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{11}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{225}{4}+\\frac{121}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{173}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{346}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{346}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543777 :question/problem "![](images/b7/b7794ce7bf86aa14413b940e250fe4c3f07281375a86bac58274e030eef5d97c.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $\\frac{5}{2}$ and $\\frac{9}{2}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{9}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}+\\frac{81}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{53}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{106}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{106}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543790 :question/problem "![](images/10/10bfa8282f54053e0ec26a51754a90ac3c21cff7dd084419fa1838209c6cad51.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $\\frac{23}{3}$ and $\\frac{11}{4}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{23}{3}}^{2}+{\\frac{11}{4}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{529}{9}+\\frac{121}{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9553}{144}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9553}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{9553}}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543792 :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a0ad3faf8dfa0944c8b43c3c5b6c4241f485cb2242aa5a5628c5f0cd7dbf568.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $\\frac{19}{4}$ and $\\frac{5}{3}:$ \n\n![](images/13/1344f3eb2a0c530da734ae8d72e30a776d5ae4e2e8be93f24a1c123f257a5e5c.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{19}{4}}^{2}+{\\frac{5}{3}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{361}{16}+\\frac{25}{9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3649}{144}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3649}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{3649}}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543794 :question/problem "![](images/64/64fad0fb0fce8784fcb390bbd01fa86558191c02677d9f7f618fa97e11787e8b.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\frac{17}{2}:$ \n\n![](images/d3/d391ea874021dc2d55e158a9a0fc5471caab15dad0dedeee16c4c9aa32066c5c.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{17}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{289}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{149}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{298}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{298}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543799 :question/problem "![](images/18/184a128aa9cd2c21049f72eff286c08d128a5fee3d9a716d984369c6fee21278.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $\\frac{17}{2}$ and $\\frac{5}{2}:$ \n\n![](images/ac/ac98d9896020e1cc8708b62d6ceeac3e7edcd2ce39ee6ef60dbbe89100b2b8d2.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{17}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{289}{4}+\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{157}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{314}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{314}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543801 :question/problem "![](images/38/388a18afaef104f8036ae6ca36786fada32fa614c724c5f5bd8f3c225a9b7286.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $2$ and $9.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+81} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{85} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{85}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{85}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543812 :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e11606627f4b91c977670cf9f919aff8b659cb5b4776cf3d04bb561b50b79fb.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{17}{2},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{19}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{23}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fa/fa1022d2a1718627898289abdd4d3035a6e06230eea16d0e46832675b2e37def.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{1}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-\\left(- \\frac{17}{2}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{19}{3}\\right)+\\frac{23}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-51)+(-38)+46}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543825 :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e11606627f4b91c977670cf9f919aff8b659cb5b4776cf3d04bb561b50b79fb.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{19}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{7}{2},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{19}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fa/fa1022d2a1718627898289abdd4d3035a6e06230eea16d0e46832675b2e37def.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{1}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{3}+\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right)+\\frac{19}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-38+(-21)+57}{6} \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543832 :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbe865077b446bdd97c290cef0924d3f17807a9e1039c9be45f4aa6e4b29cc3c.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{25}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{16}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{13}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fd/fd221ae68fc4983fb0a3f7a745154764e5c0081b2b9b66e7f8ad4a150a8b3bc5.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the inner curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are opposite to those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{25}{3}\\right)-\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{13}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-50)-32-39}{6} \\\\  & =- \\frac{121}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543834 :question/problem "![](images/12/1253965689fbeb233ca3eda553cca8ea8bced9feb81ebc6565c9b65782066b47.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{17}{2},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{19}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{1}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{3}$ is oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/ca/cad0f92fdd888243562619b410a200ca040ca43f83bfd15fb124c8a8ecae4df6.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the inner curve ${C}_{2}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{2}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}-\\left(- \\frac{19}{3}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{51-(-38)+(-2)}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
